  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "No way in",
      "title": "Bob sent a message. How does Alice find out?",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Bob’s message reaches the chat server. Alice’s phone should show it instantly.\n\nBut plain HTTP is request → response: only the **client** can start a conversation. The server has no way to call Alice’s phone and say “you’ve got mail”.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Phones and browsers sit behind home routers and mobile networks that block incoming connections — the client has to reach out."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "delay": "never",
        "rpm": "0",
        "conns": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Polling",
      "title": "Polling: keep asking",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "The simplest fix: Alice’s app asks “anything new?” every 2 seconds. Most answers are empty (pale dots); now and then one carries a message (green).\n\nEasy to build with ordinary requests — but messages arrive up to 2 seconds late, and most requests are wasted.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Polling is fine for things that change slowly — a build status, an order tracking page — with a sensible interval."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Alice polls every 2 s"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "delay": "up to 2 s",
        "rpm": "30",
        "wasted": "95%",
        "conns": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "At scale",
      "title": "Polling a million phones",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Now multiply. A million users each asking every 2 seconds is half a million requests per second — and 95% of them get the answer “nothing new”.\n\nYou’d be paying for a fleet of servers whose main job is saying no. Polling faster to cut the delay makes it worse.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "Polling trades freshness against load: halve the delay, double the requests."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Users 1 → 1,000,000",
        "~ 500,000 requests/s, 95% empty"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "delay": "up to 2 s",
        "rpm": "30",
        "wasted": "95%",
        "conns": "0"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "alice-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "srv-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bs-2": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Long polling",
      "title": "Long polling: hold the request open",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "Instead of answering “nothing” immediately, the server **holds** the request open (watch the request wait at the server) until a message arrives, or about 30 seconds pass. Then it responds, and the app immediately asks again.\n\nMessages now arrive almost instantly, and empty answers are rare.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Long polling works anywhere plain HTTP works, so it’s still used as a fallback when nothing better is available."
      },
      "changes": [
        "~ Server holds each request until there’s news",
        "~ Delay up to 2 s → under 100 ms",
        "~ Empty responses 95% → 10%"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "delay": "< 100 ms",
        "rpm": "2",
        "wasted": "10%",
        "conns": "1 held / user"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "alice-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "srv-2": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "bs-2": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "SSE",
      "title": "Server-Sent Events: one response that never ends",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "With [[Server-Sent Events]] (SSE), the app makes one request and the server never finishes the response. Instead, it writes each new event down that open stream as it happens.\n\nIt’s one-way (server → client), plain HTTP, and browsers reconnect automatically. Alice still sends her own messages with normal requests.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Ideal for feeds, notifications, live scores and dashboards — and for streaming AI responses token by token."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ One long-lived event stream to Alice",
        "~ Delay → under 50 ms"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "delay": "< 50 ms",
        "rpm": "0",
        "wasted": "0%",
        "conns": "1 per user"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "srv-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "bs-2": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "WebSockets",
      "title": "WebSockets: talk both ways",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "A [[WebSocket]] starts as an HTTP request, then upgrades into a permanent two-way connection. Either side can send a message at any moment, with tiny overhead per message.\n\nBob and Alice each hold one socket; messages flow both ways through the server in a few milliseconds.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Use WebSockets when the client talks back often: chat, multiplayer games, collaborative editing, trading screens."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Persistent two-way connections",
        "~ Sending no longer needs separate requests"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "delay": "< 20 ms",
        "rpm": "0",
        "wasted": "0%",
        "conns": "1 per user"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Many servers",
      "title": "Scaling: which server has Alice?",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "Each open connection costs memory, so a million users need many chat servers. Now Bob’s socket is on server 2, but Alice’s is on server 1 — server 2 can’t deliver to her directly.\n\nSo servers relay through [[pub/sub]]: server 2 publishes to Alice’s channel, and whichever server holds her socket is subscribed and forwards it. Watch a message take the long way round.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "This is the previous lesson again — pub/sub is how real-time systems fan messages out across servers."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Server 2",
        "+ Pub/sub between servers",
        "~ Messages relayed to the server holding each socket"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "delay": "< 30 ms",
        "rpm": "0",
        "wasted": "0%",
        "conns": "1M on 20 servers"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Offline",
      "title": "Case: Alice’s phone is offline",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Alice goes into the subway. Her socket drops, and Bob’s messages have nowhere to go.\n\nSo every message is first **saved** to a store, then pushed if the recipient is connected. When Alice reconnects, her app says “I last saw message 1041” and fetches everything after it. If she stays offline, a mobile **push notification** (APNs, FCM) wakes her phone.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Real-time delivery is an optimisation. The stored message, fetched on reconnect, is the source of truth."
      },
      "changes": [
        "- Alice offline",
        "+ Messages stored first",
        "+ Catch up on reconnect · push notification"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "delay": "< 30 ms",
        "rpm": "0",
        "wasted": "0%",
        "conns": "1M on 20 servers"
      },
      "overrides": {
